## What Changes from the Current System

| Current Prompt System | Master Schema Prompt System |
|---|---|
| Context has 7 fields | Context has 6 layers with nested intelligence |
| "Detect the technology stack" — agent guesses | Agent is told the exact framework, file locations, and rendering strategy |
| "For React/Vue/Next: create reusable schema components" — generic options list | Framework-specific instructions for the actual framework only |
| "regardless of the website's industry" — deliberately blind | Industry-specific schema types, link patterns, content formats, keyword economics |
| E.V.O. data = health score + diagnosed URLs | E.V.O. data + root cause probabilities + competitive benchmarks + cascade predictions |
| Agent decides where to start investigating | Agent is told "58% probability this is robots.txt — start there" |
| No competitive framing | "You are at the 18th percentile. Industry median is 1,200 indexed pages." |
| No platform awareness | Blocked actions explicitly listed. Agent cannot waste time on impossible recommendations. |
| Same prompt for 500-property fleet and 15,000-property fleet | Confidence-gated sections scale with fleet intelligence depth |
| "WordPress: Use Advanced Custom Fields" — irrelevant to target user | Only guidance for the actual platform appears in the prompt |

## Key Principle

The master schema transforms the prompt from a generic instruction manual into a briefing document — the kind of brief a senior consultant would write for a specialist before sending them into a client engagement. It tells the agent everything E.V.O. knows, calibrated to what it's confident about, scoped to what's actually possible on this architecture, and framed within the competitive reality of this specific industry.
